# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 2: The Woman and the Boy
#
# Zooms into ONE person from the photo of sub-lesson 1 (the busy Luxembourgish
# funfair): the woman walking in the foreground, and the boy she is holding by
# the hand. The examiner's second question on the picture task is almost always
# "pick one person and describe them" — hair, clothes, what they are doing — so
# this drills exactly that.
#
# The photo detail this describes: a young woman with dark, shoulder-length hair
# in a black top and blue denim shorts, carrying a black-and-white patterned
# handbag; next to her a boy in a green top and light shorts, smiling, holding
# her hand.
#
# Same A1–A2 constraint as sub-lesson 1, with one extra guard that appearance
# descriptions make easy to break: attributive adjectives are used ONLY on
# feminine nouns (`eng blo Box`, `eng hell Box`), where Luxembourgish leaves the
# adjective uninflected. Masculine and neuter attributives (`en schwaarzen
# T-Shirt`, `e frëndlecht Gesiicht`) need declension the learner hasn't met, so
# colours are stated predicatively instead (`D'Posch ass schwaarz a wäiss.`).
#
# Words that sub-lesson 1 already teaches (d'Fra, de Jong, droen, schwaarz, blo,
# frou, trëppelen, …) are deliberately NOT repeated here — the theme's pass-gate
# makes 01 a prerequisite for 02, and stat keys are shared across the app.

@lesson P1.02 "Describing a Picture: The Woman and the Boy"

# --- Hair ---
@word d'Hoer = the hair
@word donkel = dark
@word hell = light
@word laang = long
@word kuerz = short

# --- Clothes ---
@word den T-Shirt = the T-shirt
@word d'Box = the shorts
@word d'Jeans = the jeans
@word de Schung = the shoe
@word d'Posch = the handbag
@word wäiss = white

# --- People ---
@word d'Mamm = the mother
@word d'Hand = the hand
@word jonk = young

# --- Doing things ---
@word halen = to hold
@word lächelen = to smile
@word ausgesinn = to look like
@word nieft = next to
@word zesummen = together

@sentence
@lu Hir Hoer sinn donkel a laang.
@en Her hair is dark and long.
@distractor-lu kuerz
@distractor-lu hell
@distractor-en short
@distractor-en light

@sentence
@lu Si huet eng Posch an der Hand.
@en She has a handbag in her hand.
@distractor-lu Box
@distractor-lu dréit
@distractor-en bag
@distractor-en wears

@sentence
@lu D'Posch ass schwaarz a wäiss.
@en The handbag is black and white.
@distractor-lu blo
@distractor-lu gréng
@distractor-en blue
@distractor-en green

@sentence
@lu Si hält d'Hand vum Jong.
@en She is holding the boy's hand.
@distractor-lu kuckt
@distractor-lu Posch
@distractor-en looks
@distractor-en handbag

@sentence
@lu De Jong trëppelt nieft der Fra a lächelt.
@en The boy is walking next to the woman and smiling.
@distractor-lu hannert
@distractor-lu steet
@distractor-en behind
@distractor-en standing

@sentence
@lu De Jong dréit eng hell Box.
@en The boy is wearing light shorts.
@distractor-lu donkel
@distractor-lu Posch
@distractor-en dark
@distractor-en handbag

@sentence
@question Wéi gesäit d'Fra aus?
@lu D'Fra ass jonk an hir Hoer sinn donkel.
@en The woman is young and her hair is dark.
@distractor-lu al
@distractor-lu hell

@sentence
@question Wat dréit d'Fra?
@lu Si dréit eng blo Box an en T-Shirt.
@en She is wearing blue shorts and a T-shirt.
@distractor-lu hell
@distractor-lu Posch

@sentence
@question Wien ass d'Fra mat dem Jong?
@lu Si ass d'Mamm vum Jong.
@en She is the boy's mother.
@distractor-lu Meedchen
@distractor-lu Papp

@sentence
@question Sinn hir Hoer kuerz?
@lu Nee, hir Hoer sinn net kuerz, si sinn laang.
@en No, her hair is not short, it is long.
@distractor-lu Jo
@distractor-lu donkel

@sentence
@question Wat maachen d'Fra an de Jong?
@lu Si trëppelen zesummen op der Fouer.
@en They are walking together at the fair.
@distractor-lu eleng
@distractor-lu stinn
